The genre ignited along the mist-choked cliffs of Brittany at the Priory of Saint-Cyprian-des-Flots. Here, an apostate order of Carthusian brothers broke centuries of silence by wiring customized EEG electrodes to their temples while sleeping, funneling their nocturnal terrors through an expansive wall of Eurorack modular synthesizers. Local outcast surf-punks and industrial goths, drawn to the low-frequency rumble vibrating through the sea caves, infiltrated the chapel ruins with rusted Fender Jaguars and an alto saxophone salvaged from a sunken ferry. What emerged was a ritualistic counterculture: the Fraternité du Ressac. Adherents gather during midnight neap tides wearing brine-stiffened monk robes over black neoprene wetsuits, their faces smeared with charcoal and kelp ash. Performances are treated as sonic exorcisms, staged around portable diesel generators on jagged intertidal rocks while modular cables snake into the incoming breakers.

Typical Instruments: Fender Jaguar/Jazzmaster (wound with heavy flatwounds, pushed through modified vintage spring-reverb tanks), Alto Saxophone (fitted with internal barrel pickups and routed through granular delay processors), Custom Eurorack Modular Systems (featuring complex oscillators, stochastic burst generators, and voltage-controlled filters), Roland TR-909 modified for micro-glitch manipulation, and harmonium/chapel pipe organs.

